WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.360
How did a real-life cowboy go from rodeo&nbsp;
shows to appearing in "Midnight, Texas?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.960
How did that same actor land on just about every&nbsp;
Western and quasi-Western movie and television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:17.040
show of the last 20 years? Let's dive into&nbsp;
the long and varied career of Forrie J. Smith.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:24.320
There's a reason why the wise, rugged Lloyd Pierce&nbsp;
on "Yellowstone" imbues his scenes with such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.600
air of realism. As it turns out, the actor behind&nbsp;
the long-time Dutton employee, Forrie J. Smith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:36.160
is a real-life cowboy with decades of experience&nbsp;
under his large-buckled belt. That experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.680
served him well as both a stunt man and an actor,&nbsp;
and throughout his decades-long career in show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.840
business, Smith has been back in the saddle more&nbsp;
times than one could count. This, perhaps, is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.760
the charismatic and occasionally mysterious Lloyd&nbsp;
Pierce looks so familiar to viewers. Beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.800
in the late 1980s, the actor found himself&nbsp;
starring in several television movies, TV series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.040
and films, and in each one managed to prove&nbsp;
that, as a performer, he was no one-trick pony.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.920
"No room for outcasts like us in the bunkhouse."

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.480
By 1993, Forrie J. Smith had several off-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.720
and minor roles under his belt. He was&nbsp;
mostly known for his work as a stuntman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.680
in "Rambo III" when he took on the role of&nbsp;
Pony Deal in writer Kevin Jarre's "Tombstone."

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.840
The film told the story of gunslingers&nbsp;
and occasional lawmen Wyatt Earp and Doc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.320
Holliday's involvement in the infamous 1881&nbsp;
shoot-out at the O.K. Corral in Tombstone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.920
Arizona. The movie's cast was stacked with&nbsp;
A-listers, but even among the superstars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.320
Smith managed to make an impression—with&nbsp;
future casting directors, at least.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.800
Though Smith's role in the film was as a&nbsp;
non-speaking ensemble member of the criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.920
Clanton Gang, the film's success helped the&nbsp;
experienced stunt man showcase his stage skills.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.080
Four years after his role as a rambunctious&nbsp;
cowboy in "Tombstone," Forrie J. Smith starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.280
alongside Javier Bardem, Rosie Perez, and James&nbsp;
Gandolfini in director Álex de la Iglesia's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.920
critically acclaimed dark comedy, "Perdita&nbsp;
Durango." Strutting onto the scene, Smith's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.480
Armendariz attempts to negotiate with a fellow&nbsp;
criminal before being killed in a shoot-out.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:16.640
Although the scene is relatively short, it allowed&nbsp;
Smith to fully embrace and embody a character with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.640
some semblance of a backstory. The film's cult&nbsp;
status helped the already prolific and in-demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.160
"additional crew" cowboy flex his acting chops.&nbsp;
The year prior, Smith had starred in a short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.640
scene in the made-for-TV movie, "Ruby Jean&nbsp;
and Joe," wherein he delivered a few drunken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.880
lines at a birthday party before getting&nbsp;
punched in the face by Tom Selleck. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.080
it was hardly as big an on-screen moment for&nbsp;
the actor as his shoot-out in Perdita Durango.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.680
Though Forrie J. Smith's screen time in Duncan&nbsp;
Tucker's Oscar-nominated "Transamerica" was short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.200
his dialogue in the scene acts as a catalyst for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.320
critical moment for the film's&nbsp;
protagonists. In the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.680
a religious, conservative woman named Bree, who&nbsp;
is saving up for sex reassignment surgery, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.560
compelled by her therapist to track down the son&nbsp;
she unknowingly fathered while still in college.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.600
Bree heads to New York to track&nbsp;
down her son, but since she is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.600
unable to explain the true nature of her&nbsp;
relationship to the street-hustler teen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.160
she pretends to be a Christian case-worker.&nbsp;
The two embark on a cross-country journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.320
back to L.A. and, along the way, stop at a&nbsp;
roadside market in Arizona called Sammy's Wigwam.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.240
Smith plays Sammy, who assumes the&nbsp;
angsty teen is the woman's child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.800
and when Toby swears at Bree,&nbsp;
Smith's character chastises him.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.640
"You watch your mouth around your mother."

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.480
"She is not my mother. she's not anyone's&nbsp;
mother, she's not even a real woman."

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.960
It's an important moment in developing the&nbsp;
characters' relationship and vindicates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.320
Bree's fears, and her motivations for lying to&nbsp;
Toby, while illustrating that the two very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.080
already come off as a parent and child. Smith's&nbsp;
success following his role in "Transamerica"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.280
is proof that sometimes it's more about quality&nbsp;
than quantity when it comes to screen-time.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.360
By 2017, Forrie J. Smith had established&nbsp;
himself as the man to call when casting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.720
directors were looking for a real-life wrangler&nbsp;
and horseman who also knew how to deliver a line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.600
In 2013, he provided his animal wrangler&nbsp;
skills to the crew of "2 Guns." In 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.520
he worked with future "Yellowstone" creator&nbsp;
Taylor Sheridan as an animal wrangler on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.800
Oscar-nominated "Hell or High Water." While&nbsp;
one would be hard-pressed to recognize Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.600
in either film, he had a more visible role in&nbsp;
the "Breaking Bad" spin-off, "Better Call Saul."

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.120
In the Season 3 episode "Mabel," Smith stars&nbsp;
as the junkyard owner of Five J's Auto Salvage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.360
who loans Mike some tools and space to work.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.840
In 2018, Forrie J. Smith lent his characteristic&nbsp;
Western aesthetic to NBC's "Midnight, Texas."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.560
The town of Midnight is a safe place for&nbsp;
outsiders, who fit the category mainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:52.400
based on their supernatural characteristics. Among&nbsp;
that cast of outsiders is former "weretiger," the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.280
now-cured Reverend Emilio Sheehan. In the&nbsp;
episode "To Witch Hell and Back," viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.480
learn the origin of Sheehan's identity and watch&nbsp;
as he struggles to come to terms with his past.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.520
Smith appears in the flashback as the real&nbsp;
Reverend Emilio Sheehan, a priest and cowboy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.880
who was killed by the transformed weretiger years&nbsp;
ago. When the weretiger transformed into his human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.320
self again, he took on the priest's identity,&nbsp;
wracked with guilt and determined to atone.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.600
Smith's real-life experience clearly helps&nbsp;
him get across the rugged realism of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.320
characters he plays. That seems to be true&nbsp;
whether he's being attacked by a weretiger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.960
or doing the Dutton's dirty work on "Yellowstone."

